[{:seon.task/name "roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Flow: root's procs as the first fork's graph, started in root, none in the system process; the commit stream to the system process as one ordered Flow path, a drain answered. Flow's answer to runtime was sent 2026-11-16 (the fork point single-threaded, the writer process holding every proc). SPLIT (agreed): the first-notice load in seon.fork-server/serve is runtime's, with flow's proof seon.procs-test/a-bare-fork-server-takes-main-from-its-first-notice; one collection per notice that moved the program, after the load, never before a fork; test children forked by the system process at main's program, loading their branch's diff (tests2, seon.prove/tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent). NAMES (owner): the system process is the binary's main, the fork server and every agent's parent; the writer process is its first fork (today's root: the writer, the api, the reconciler). THE STOP (deps, in seon.main/the-binary-main-is-the-system-process): the writer process ending with :stop or :hand-over ends the system process's group and the process; a crash of the writer process or its restart on the same binary leaves the system process and its agents running and forks a new writer process. FLOW'S NEXT ITEM: the agent's :transport proc (seon.repl/transport-step) reconnects instead of ending on the writer process's EOF: each wait on it answered typed :seon.system/restarting, then a reconnect to the writer process through the system process's listener (:seon.server/root-port in the agent's start map, deps) and the waits re-asked; the new writer process adopting an agent it did not spawn is runtime's side, to agree. MEASURE (deps, corrected): an agent's own memory is its private dirty bytes (vmmap SM=PRV dirty; jolt.host/process-private-bytes, read inside the process), never phys_footprint, which also counts dirty pages still shared copy-on-write with the parent (deps: a fresh agent's 225 MB footprint = 52.1 MB its own + 172.9 MB shared; flow's 626 / 646 / 652 MB footprints overstate the same way). The 5 MB row is held to private bytes, read in one eval after the fork, after (System/gc), after one read. STATE 2026-11-16 (flow, at compaction): ON MAIN: the hand-over op, L2 step 1 (restarting), the stop fact inside the drain, the stall detector deleted (the drain has no bound now), B1 (:call frame, :write names no branch), G2 (seon.agent/*start*), :seon.task/cause deleted. ON jolt/flow, unmerged: docs/flow.md 'Every proc is seen' and 'Every process is a Flow process'. NEXT, in order: (1) web's Flow view (seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow, web builds, flow reviews): flow's parts are two agent transport ops, {:op :flow-graph} (datafy of its graph) and {:op :flow-ping} (one ping), and the graph's report and error chans as a mult the api's :back and web tap; deps asked for two Jolt changes (a transition and a parked put announced on the report chan). Upstream flow-monitor is cloned at reference-code/core.async.flow-monitor. (2) G1: the transport's :waits into its proc (no stall decision); G3: the :repl fields; G4: a :branch proc; then the atom deleted; B2: the writer process gets the :repl proc, agent=root lands there, seon.server/answer's :root branch deleted. (3) the agent reconnect (deps' :seon.server/root-port). (4) arm-wall! as a test's estimate deadline (with tests2); bin/stalls and bin/evidence. (5) the 17 s drain (not reproduced: the last stop on record, runs/root.log.prev, took 423 ms, drain 70 ms and flow-stop 345 ms; root to name where 17.4 s was read). THE STOP-START PING RECORD (root's yes 2026-11-16): PRIOR WORK: stop! prints jolt.host/fiber-dump at its start (system.clj:2441), proved in its log by seon.system-test/a-root-resumes-a-store-an-earlier-build-wrote; Jolt's flow/ping already answers each proc's status, count, state, ::flow/site and ::flow/in-fill, and ::flow/unanswered and ::flow/report-dropped as metadata, waiting only on procs not mid-step (reference-code/jolt flow/impl.clj:391-434); web's agreed snapshot read (seon.web/the-system-seen-from-flow, interface (2)) is that answer; searched src for flow/ping and seon.graph for a ping, snapshot or status function: none. DESIGN: seon.graph/snapshot, the snapshot read web's view and the stop share: Jolt's ping answer with each proc's ::flow/ins and ::flow/outs left out (channel objects, the topology being datafy's), its metadata kept; stop! prints it beside the fiber dump. COST: n = 79 procs on root [M]; per proc one control put and one pong, ~900 ticks -> ~71k ticks, 25 KB printed. MEASURED at root's REPL: 4.1-7.0 ms, 71k-84k ticks, 24,775 chars [result/cf3b1fd2, result/ad8995ba]. RISK, named to root: ping's timeout (1000 ms) is a clock on our own procs; it bounds the stop's start only when an idle proc fails to answer, and that proc is then named under ::flow/unanswered. LESSONS: search the codebase and reference-code before building (flow-view duplicated web's task); run bin/laws on the merged tip and bin/build seon before a READY that touches system.edn, schemas or deletes a namespace; a deleted schema key's cites in tasks break the seed; never hold-start! at root's REPL; no long foreground evals (short timeouts). THE AGENT PROCESS REBUILT AS A FLOW GRAPH (the owner's order, 2026-11-16, after flow's audit against upstream core.async-flow doc/flow-guide.md and flow-monitor): G2 was wrong: it put the start values in a process-wide var (seon.agent/*start*, alter-var-root), state outside Flow like the atom it replaced; it broke every agent and ~60 test sites. FLOW'S RULES: a step holds no state outside its state map and touches no channel (it returns outputs); init takes the flow def's :args; the transition arity opens and releases resources; outside data enters through ::flow/in-ports. MEASURED NOW: one atom seon.agent/state of 30 fields watched by ctx, 10 start fields in *start*, 3 procs [result/a439ce47]; health's :stateful-outside-flow 70 rows on main, nearly all the agent's [result/dd0d0e2d]; a trivial eval 17k ticks, 263 KB [result/ba606235]. PRIOR WORK: seon.repl/agent-steps and serve! (the graph), seon.agent/await-reply and send! (the socket), seon.repl/drive-run (a run's effects), seon.step/run-loop (the fork server's second runtime), docs/flow.md#flow-inside-an-agent. PHASES, each landed red-first and small: P1 :transport owns root's socket both ways and the waits: send! becomes an output to it, await-reply's ask is registered and sent in its one step, the link and :waits leave the atom; no other proc or thread writes the socket. P2 start values as :args to each proc, each init keeping its fields; the :repl proc binds the agent's identity around the eval it runs; *start* and hold-start! deleted; a test makes an agent proc by calling its init with args (my.test/a-test-agent-is-made-one-way folds in, the ~20 hand-made harnesses deleted). P3 the :repl proc owns the REPL and the branch (one writer: :tests sends its results to it to commit); code an eval runs works on the step's own context and the step returns it as the new state; the ctx watch becomes the :repl proc's reaction to its state; the atom deleted. P4 a run's effects and test children are declared worker procs, never ad-hoc fibers or threads; serve!'s error loop becomes a proc. P5 (with runtime) the fork server is a one-proc graph on its one thread, seon.step/run-loop deleted. STATE AT COMPACTION (2026-11-16 ~20:00): ON MAIN: the writer takes its requests' answers (a6f2e04e5a); bin/stalls, evidence, bounded, end-tree deleted (a41c9db9dd); the reconciler's :gate stage deleted and seon_done's :judge running judge! with reach? true, take 5 gone (40ec87eb17). IN FLIGHT on jolt/flow: a WIP commit (a helper's, not reviewed) deleting the checkout prove chain: seon.fx run-on-checkout, run-held, the :tests effect and :fx/tests owner, gate-answer; seon.system's :gate op and :gate dest arm and prove-candidate!; seon.writer's :merge-candidate; judge!'s gate machinery (ask-gate, send-gate!, gate-later!, apply-gate!); judgment making a merge task :ready with its process tests named, not run; the config keys :seon.config.source/gate-checkout and tests-dir whole (runtime reviews the seon.config hunk). Next: review every line, prove at a fresh agent (reached in-process tests), one build, READY. ROOT'S RULING (after compaction): the :tests effect (:fx/tests, fx/run-tests, full-run) and :seon.config.source/tests-dir are deleted too, with the daily full run (a clock and a whole-suite run); tests2 deletes seon.cron and seon.prove's :full. CORRECTED by tests2: flow writes the :full deletion too (Flow code), in the same commit as the :tests effect, one landing. Its list: the :cron proc (system.edn:38-39), system.clj's :cron-tick and :full-run-at binds (~1871) and [seon.cron] in its requires (~26); src/seon/cron.clj and test/seon/cron_test.clj; seon.prove's full-request (~329), full-answered (~348), the (= :full mode) branch (~536), the :full answer (~654) and the key's \"!full\" (~39); fx/full-run-command (~1000); :seon.config.proofs/full-run-at's schema entry (seon.config.proofs.edn:12-14) and its seon.config.edn line (~122); prove_test's three :full tests (~150, 172, 182); docs clocks.md:24, config.md:80 (day-ms), root.md:14, parity.md:61 (scheduled work: deleted, the judge's one prove by reach does it); source_test.clj:268 names src/seon/cron.clj as an input file: swap in another src file. Deletes line: \"the daily full run; what does its job: the judge's one prove by reach.\" Verify each line number with git grep first. FLOW AUDIT (flow, 2026-11-16, root's order, against upstream core.async-flow read whole: a proc's state is init's return from the flow def's :args (impl.clj:263, args from :procs at 38-48 and 155), a loop local threaded through transition and transform (impl.clj:271-322), never anywhere else; the outside reads it only by ping over the control channel (impl.clj:76-86, 272-279; flow-monitor flow_monitor.clj:70-81 pings each second and holds only the flow handle, :88-98 datafy, inject, pause and resume); flow-guide.md:9 step-fns hold no state and touch no channel, :37 transition opens and releases resources, :51 outside data enters by ::flow/in-ports. RUN at root's REPL: a proc given {:agent {:key a1}} as :args, read back by ping as its ::flow/state with count 3 [result/a72a3832]; the step var redefined mid-run (a reload) and the state kept, n 2 to 3 [result/c6a02a29]; :ping-map-fn is fixed when the process launches (impl.clj:246), so a reload does not change what ping shows. THE AGENT'S PROCS (seon.repl, seon.agent): (A1) agent.clj:34 seon.agent/state, one process-wide atom of ~30 fields that all three procs, every eval and seon.ctx read and write (81 sites in agent.clj, 53 in repl.clj, 17 in turn.clj, 10 in my/agent.clj): no proc owns it; ctx.clj:975 add-watch on it at namespace load and ctx.clj:784 the ctx atom derived from it. (A2) agent.clj:36-47 *start* and hold-start! (alter-var-root): a process-wide var holding what the store already records (:seon.agent/key unique identity, :seon.agent/branch, :seon.agent/task, result/b035ba07) plus the connection and the graph. (A3) agent.clj:49 link, a defonce atom holding the socket; agent.clj:61-66 send! writes the socket from any proc, eval or fiber (repl.clj:1367, 1458, 1472, 1515, 1557, 1615) instead of an output of one proc. (A4) transport-step repl.clj:1541 init reads the global intake (agent.clj:68, link); :1540 ping-map-fn and :1546, 1551 read and deliver promises held in the global state's :waits; :1554 lib/c-exit inside a step; :1558-1565 the step drives its own graph (pause-proc, resume-proc, drain) through the global graph handle. (A5) agent-step repl.clj:1419 ping-map-fn reads the global :n; serve-request :1441-1476 mutates global :busy, writes the socket, commits the store (I/O in an :io step) and :1445 c-exit; drive-run :1393 starts an io-thread from a step to put the effect's answer on the proc's own port. (A6) tests-step repl.clj:1513-1514 runs a whole test run in one step (handle! -> run-tests-forked -> run-children!, agent.clj:1628 a/<!! on a promise-chan: a step blocked outside its channels) and reads the :repl proc's :busy and :dirty? from the global atom; serve-item! :1486-1489 two io-threads per item. (A7) test-run-step agent.clj:1567+ forks children in its ::flow/resume transition and edits its own graph from a step (flow/update! :1536, 1549, through *start*'s graph); :1474 an io-thread per child blocks on its exit and puts onto the proc's port. (A8) await-reply agent.clj:262-273: a promise in the global :waits, a put-after! timer and a go block, then a blocking deref inside whatever step called it (merge-main!, fx!, prove!, clone!), so steps wait on root outside channels. (A9) serve! repl.clj:1606-1618 the graph's error channel read by a hand loop outside Flow that mutates the global atom and writes the socket; run! :1637-1644 the hello built from globals. SEON.STEP: (S1) step.clj:66-94 run-loop, a second process loop beside Flow's (pause, ping, drain and stop as data on a transport), used by the fork server: two runtimes for one contract. (S2) step.clj:39-59 timer, arm!, fired!: a volatile kept in proc state and mutated by the step, made to survive Flow keeping the old state when a step throws; the fix is the throw's cause, not state outside the return. (S3) step.clj:96-99 out!: a step builds its outputs in a volatile instead of returning them. (S4) step.clj:124-157 deliver-all!, end-all, ask-edge: answers are promises delivered from steps and derefed by callers outside the graph (ask-edge's blocking >!! and deref), the edge into a proc from outside; Flow's way is inject or an in-port and the answer as an output. (S5) step.clj:22-32 put-after! is the one timer, a clock putting into a proc's channel; right for a declared deadline on the world outside, wrong for a wait on our own procs (await-reply's bound, A8). ROOT'S PROCS: see the next paragraph. ROOT'S PROCS (a Sonnet helper's sweep, 17 citations checked by flow): every proc reads config through the process-wide held atom (config.clj:355, cfg/value) and the wall clock: (R0) the config a proc reads is not its args. (R1) api-proc system.clj:1016: :graph and :system are promises given as :args and derefed in init (:1032) and steps (:572, 1063, flow/update! from a step); callers' promises held in :pending, ::waits, :shutdown-p and delivered (:162, 556, 707-710, 886-933); a ::taken volatile (:992, 1035, 1051); a/poll! on :back and :ext held in state (:1055) and a/close! of :to-writer in a step (:1068); put-after! on :back (:705); agents' sockets and remote ends opened in a step (:355-359) not a transition; stop (:1044) answers :pending and ::waits only, leaving ::spawning, ::killers, ::backing, ::respawn and ::held-spawns. (R2) writer-proc writer.clj:575: init transacts on the store (:589); blocking store calls in steps (:143, 439-490, 530, 602, 619; task the-writers-store-calls-are-an-effect); a/poll! of :to-writer (:557); the transition is st (:595): its :pend batch and :pending asks unanswered at stop. flusher-proc :644 blocking sync (:655). (R3) fork server: server-step fork_server.clj:280 runs on seon.step/run-loop (:382), not Flow (S1); open and ready volatiles (:267, 314-318, 370-374); blocking alts!! and waitpid (:375, 23, 308); fork-server-proc :503 starts a reader Thread in init (:395, 512) sharing the socket with steps that send on it (:427, 529, 543); a/<!! and .join in a transition (:454-461). (R4) reconciler-proc reconcile.clj:1467: :judge promises held and delivered (:1321, 1458); clocks (:858-1520). (R5) prove-proc prove.clj:650: init reads the global program/binary-program (:664); the transition is st (:666), its :runs waiters left to the api's end-all. (R6) render-proc render/proc.clj:1365: binding seon.ratom/*owner* around steps (:844, 857, 1244) and an :rv-owner of volatiles other owners dirty (:842); :graph-p and :system-p promises (:60, 91) and a blocking ping of the whole graph from a step (:91); a/put! to its own :health-wake (:857, 995, 1245, 1442), a/tap in init (:1386). (R7) web-proc web.clj:494: the listener bound in init (:515) not a transition; edge promises in state (:444, 485, 525); a/offer! and close! on tab channels from steps (:294-399). (R8) owner-proc fx.clj:1783: :tick-timer volatile (S2; :1803, 1647, 1824); load-average and ps in a step (~70 ms, :1666-1667); the transition is st (:1806): :queue, :flight and :pending unanswered and the timer armed at stop. worker-proc :1940: a Thread. started from a step (:1857, 1991); open and perform inline, blocking (:1974-1978); stop never calls :close (:1958-1961). (R9) agent-proc agent_proc.clj:308: blocking sock/send! of an fd and kill! in steps (:68, 127-151); stop sends :stop and awaits nothing (:330); its end opened by the api, not by it. (R10) system.edn :bind/conn hands one live Datahike connection to 8 procs (edn :19-55), :bind/graph and :bind/system are promises delivered after the procs exist (system.clj:1872, 1878), and boot-made channels are read directly by procs. COUNT: root 113 rows (global 17, state not owned 21, channel in a step 14, wait outside channels 17, thread 5, init not from args 5, transition 14), the agent's A1-A9 and seon.step's S1-S5 above. THE FIX PLAN (flow, 2026-11-16, sent to root; each step red first and small; the owner: a proc's state is the proc's, the database records what an agent is, nothing global): (F1, first) *start* and hold-start! deleted whole (A2). The store already records what an agent is (:seon.agent/key unique identity, :seon.agent/branch, :seon.agent/task): each agent proc's :args are {:key k :role r} (role :agent or :test), its init keeps them, and its store connection is opened in its own init or transition, never handed over; task and branch are read from the agent's entity on the proc's db. A function that read *start* takes what it reads (key, conn, db) from the calling step's state as arguments. An eval: the :repl proc passes its state to eval-string, which evaluates the form with the local agent bound to that value, and the REPL's names (my.*) take it as their first argument, passed by the REPL as it resolves them; no var, no binding, no atom, so a reload touches nothing. :pause, :resume and :drain stop driving the graph from a step (A4): the :repl proc narrows its own ::flow/input-filter (Flow's way, flow.clj:228-232), so no proc holds the graph. Proof red first: an agent proc made by calling its init with {:key k} answers its key and task from the db, red under a stand-in that reads nothing; a namespace reload at a running agent keeps its key. F1 BUILT on jolt/flow (root approved 2026-11-16): the agent value is {:key :role :conn :cfg :serve :graph}; the connection stays one, opened by init!, since the :tests proc still writes the branch through it (results) until F3 moves those writes to :repl, and two connections would be two writers on one branch; the graph promise stays for :transport's pause, resume and drain and for run-children!'s fold procs until F3 makes a run a message, so input-filter pause moves to F3. An eval: seon.repl/eval-as compiles (fn [agent] (let [name (partial #'var agent) ...] form)) for a form naming agent or an unqualified REPL name whose contract takes :seon.agent/start first (seon.agent/takes-agent?); a qualified call passes agent itself. Measured at a fresh agent: a form with no such name +2.1k ticks and 9 KB over eval (52.4k, 686 KB) [result/a1a4d2c5], after a find-var prefilter (repl-name alone was 22k ticks and 100 KB a symbol, result/cddca010); proofs red under stand-ins and green: seon.repl-test/an-agents-procs-and-evals-get-the-agent-as-a-value (6/6, 1.1M ticks) and seon.problems-test/a-branch-that-adds-a-dynamic-var-or-sets-a-root-fails-unless-it-names-its-edge (3/3, 11.9M ticks). (F2) the process-test runner in the prove proc, the candidate its state (queue item 0). (F3) the state atom split into the procs that own each field (A1): :transport owns the socket and the waits (A3, A4, A8: send! an output to it, await-reply a request whose answer is a message to the asking proc, no promise, no clock on our own procs); :repl owns db, base, main, the REPL and the branch's one writer (A5); a test run and its children are procs, a batch a message (A6, A7); serve!'s error loop a proc (A9); seon.ctx's atom and watch become a reaction of :repl's state. (F4) seon.step: out! goes, a step returns its outputs (S3); the timer volatile goes with the throw's cause (S2); promises become replies on the asker's in-port or flow/inject (S4); put-after! kept only for a declared deadline on the world outside (S5); run-loop deleted when the fork server is a one-proc graph (S1, with runtime). (F5) root's procs: config a value in each proc's :args, derived again on a config transaction (R0); the :bind promises and the shared connection go: the graph's owner alone changes it, on a message (R1, R6, R10), and the writer alone holds the connection, others take db references (R2, R4-R6, R9, R10); every transition answers what its proc holds and opens or closes its resources (R1-R3, R5, R7-R9). QUEUE AFTER IT (root's order: critical infrastructure only): (0) the process-test runner as the prove proc's own (the owner: tests run in a flow): the booted candidate is the prove proc's state, booted once per candidate commit, and each batch a message to it; no *candidate* var (refused). Spec, inputs, state, the batch message, outputs and three proofs with their red stand-ins: task seon.prove/tests-fork-from-the-branchs-warm-agent (jolt/tests2 b7cfc9fdfa); reference code f91eddfcd0 and 0778373bb2 (reverted on jolt/tests2, readable in git). Comes after *start* in the fix plan; (1) this rebuild; (2) ask-main, main-answer, reds-to-ask, the judge flight's :main-asked and judge-tx's main-red producers deleted, landed with context's done-rule half (jolt/context fc86fde8c1, which does not compile alone: take it with context's next status commit, which restores also-on-main as the page's fact only and moves status onto proof facts), plus reconcile_test's ask-main tests and seon.proofs/main-red with its caller (agent.clj ~1977); (3) one search service (below); (4) slow-test helpers: reconcile-test (32, after the WIP lands), step-test, procs-test. FINDINGS: seon.writer/unanalyzable refuses nothing inside a merge (refresh-tx analyzes only forms that differ from the db given; data has it, result/cb93fa3d); seon.proofs/ranking and spent-ms hold unnamed bounds (tests2's), so seon.main-test/a-bound-is-a-config-key is red. ALSO IN THE REBUILD (decided 2026-11-16): (i) a branch's process stays current by evaluating each update of main as it arrives (decisions: a branch holds its code and stays current), so an agent cannot run old main by construction; (ii) DONE on main (097c515188): embedding tracks main only; ``seon.agent/embed-written!``, its turn call and the agent's indexing state deleted. (iii) tests2's agent-side items: root's :judge proves the merged branch (prove-merged!, tests2 a8811d6273); the run writes no verdict files and reads typical-ms and slow-tests through seon.proofs/spent-ms (43ebd0e5b6); own-results-first and record-results!'s :seon.test/* shape deleted once context's and web's readers move; ask-gate and send-gate! deleted with root's NOW plan's :gate stage; (iv) two main reds in heard-main's path, fixed or rewritten to the behaviour here, patched by nobody before: seon.agent-test/a-message-taken-by-one-agent-is-still-owed-to-the-other and a-private-branch-is-owed-a-message-until-its-own-act-delivers-it (agent/undelivered answers [] on main); (v) after the rebuild: the one searcher on the :vectors proc (a search request held until the proc is caught up past its :at, answered in the step that catches it up, the drain answering held ones :seon.system/restarting; no timer), with context's backfill change and the callers moved, and the search row's measured cost brought down. (vi) dropped: runtime reverted test-held! (jolt/runtime 1f4d9e88d8); a test child takes its config by role from its start map. (vii) the start map, agreed with runtime: {:role :agent|:test|:system :id :key :task :branch :store-conn :graph <promise> :process {:ports {:server :web} :store :runs :main :source-root :checkout :cores :end-with :embed-model}}, :role replacing :mode; fixed paths (system-path, tasks-dir, public-dir, cwd, generations-dir) are derived from :checkout and :runs, never keys. Config is runtime's pure fn (seon.config/of-branch db role): schema defaults, then the branch's :config/value entities, then the role's profile; the :repl proc calls it and re-derives in the step whose commit touches config datoms. Until the rebuild lands, runtime's seams seon.config/process and seon.config/role read argv once and (:mode *start*); the rebuild repoints them at the start map. (viii) web's seon.agent/an-agents-merge-base-is-a-reference: :base, :main and :db held in the :repl proc's state as points {:branch :commit :t}, opened where read (web's sites, result/a3148031). COST BOUND: start values given once (0 bytes, 0 ticks a message after init); each field in one proc's state, an update the step's return (no atom, watch or CAS); a message one Flow dispatch plus its work; a reply one output and one frame written once; an eval one binding; no thread beyond Flow's; a fresh agent's private bytes <= 5 MB. FLOW'S OTHER CENSUS ITEM (docs/flow.md#every-process-is-a-flow-process): seon.main's stdin thread (an io-thread reading stdin for its EOF) goes when the system process owns start and stop: the writer process's end is a message from it, never a read of its stdin."
